{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it was good.</b> He dealt well with them. And this is a distinction for a word whose root is two letters, and a vav-yud is placed at its beginning when it comes to express the hif'il conjugation — the yud is vowelized with tzeirei, which is a small kamatz, as in ״וַיֵּיטֶב אֱלֹהִים לַמְיַלְּדֹת״, ״וַיֶּרֶב בְּבַת יְהוּדָה״ (Lamentations 2) — he multiplied lamentation. And likewise ״וַיֶּגֶל הַשְּׁאֵרִית״ of Nevuzaradan (II Chronicles 36) — he exiled the remnant; ״וַיֶּפֶן זָנָב אֶל זָנָב״ (Judges 15) — he turned the tails toward each other; all of these are in the conjugation of causing others to act. But when it expresses the nif'al conjugation, the yud is vowelized with chiriq, as in ״וַיִּיטַב בְּעֵינָיו״ (Leviticus 10) — it was pleasing; and likewise ״וַיִּרֶב הָעָם״ — the people multiplied; ״וַיִּגֶל יְהוּדָה״ (II Kings 25) — Judah was exiled; ״וַיִּפֶן כֹּה וָכֹה״ (Exodus 2:12) — he turned this way and that. And do not answer me with וַיֵּלֶךְ, וַיֵּשֶׁב, וַיֵּרֶד, וַיֵּצֵא, for they are not derived from the same class as these, since the yud is part of their root: יֵלֵךְ, יֵשֵׁב, יֵרֵד, יֵצֵא — yod is the third letter in them." "<b>And Elohim dealt well with the midwives.</b> What was the goodness?"], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:50:12.895759883Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}